---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import profile from '../../data/agile-profile.json';

const OCTOPUS_REFERRAL = 'https://share.octopus.energy/lush-spark-49';
const AXLE_REFERRAL = 'https://vpp.axle.energy/landing?ref=R-QADMOHUO';

const slots = profile.slots as number[];
const lo = Math.min(...slots);
const hi = Math.max(...slots);
const slotTime = (i: number) => `${String(Math.floor(i / 2)).padStart(2, '0')}:${i % 2 ? '30' : '00'}`;
// 16:00–19:00 is the evening peak the battery exists to dodge; ink it so it reads as the villain.
const isPeak = (i: number) => i >= 32 && i < 38;
const shade = (v: number) => {
  const k = (v - lo) / (hi - lo);
  if (k > 0.72) return '#5b83e6';
  if (k > 0.45) return '#9ab4f2';
  if (k > 0.22) return '#c9d8f8';
  return '#e8eefc';
};
const cheapest = slots.indexOf(lo);
const dearest = slots.indexOf(hi);
---

<Base title="The tariff" description="Why this UK solar, battery and Predbat home runs Octopus Agile import with fixed 12p export, and what happened when Octopus Flux closed a month before our install.">
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>The tariff</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Agile import, half-hourly, re-priced every afternoon for the following day. Fixed 12p export
      is lined up for the moment certification lands. The tariff matters every bit as much as the
      hardware, because it decides what every stored kWh is actually worth.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="card">
    <div class="card-head">
      <h2>A typical day's import price</h2>
      <span class="meta">mean of a full year of published rates · Jun 2025 → Jun 2026</span>
    </div>
    <div class="price-curve">
      {slots.map((v, i) => (
        <span
          class="bar"
          style={`height:${((v - lo * 0.72) / (hi - lo * 0.72)) * 100}%; background:${isPeak(i) ? 'var(--dark)' : shade(v)}`}
          title={`${slotTime(i)} — ${v.toFixed(2)}p`}
        ></span>
      ))}
    </div>
    <div class="price-axis"><span>00:00</span><span>06:00</span><span>12:00</span><span>18:00</span><span>23:30</span></div>
    <p class="caption">
      The battery charges in the pale hours and carries the house through the near-black
      16:00–19:00 peak. That gap is where most of the saving comes from — more than the panels, on
      a bad week. Averaged over a full year of published rates the cheapest half hour is
      <strong>{lo.toFixed(2)}p at {slotTime(cheapest)}</strong> and the dearest
      <strong>{hi.toFixed(2)}p at {slotTime(dearest)}</strong>, with a mean of
      {' '}<strong>{profile.mean_p_per_kwh}p</strong>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="cols-2" style="margin-top:1rem">
    <div class="card referral">
      <p class="kicker">Thinking of switching?</p>
      <h2 style="margin:0.2rem 0 0"><img src="/octopus-mark.png" alt="" class="octopus-logo-inline" /> Octopus · we both get £50</h2>
      <p>No sponsors on this site. The referral links are the only money it makes, and they pay you
      exactly what they pay us. You don't need solar — any tariff counts.</p>
      <div class="cta">
        <a class="btn octopus" href={OCTOPUS_REFERRAL} rel="noopener"><img src="/octopus-mark.png" alt="Octopus Energy" class="octopus-mark" /> Claim £50</a>
        <p class="fine mono">share.octopus.energy/lush-spark-49</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="card referral">
      <p class="kicker">Got a battery already?</p>
      <h2 style="margin:0.2rem 0 0"><img src="/axle-logo-black.png" alt="" class="axle-logo-inline" /> Axle · we both get £25</h2>
      <p>Paid per grid-balancing event at £1/kWh, £10 a month guaranteed. Events-only mode keeps
      Predbat in charge the rest of the time. <a href="/stats/#axle">What it's actually paid us →</a></p>
      <div class="cta">
        <a class="btn axle" href={AXLE_REFERRAL} rel="noopener"><img src="/axle-logo-black.png" alt="Axle" class="axle-mark" /> Claim £25</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>The Flux plot twist</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    The original business case was built on <strong>Octopus Flux</strong>, the classic battery
    tariff with a cheap overnight window and a 30p evening export peak. The plan was dead simple:
    charge cheap overnight, force-export the battery into the 4pm to 7pm peak, profit. Then in
    <strong>March 2026</strong> Flux and Intelligent Octopus Flux closed to new customers, months
    before our panels went on the roof.
  </p>

  <div class="note warn">
    <p><strong>Lesson one:</strong> don't pin a multi-year payback sum on one tariff sticking
    around. Tariffs are products, and products get pulled. Model your second-best option too. We
    hadn't, and re-planning under time pressure was no fun at all.</p>
  </div>

  <p class="lede">
    Here's the surprise: losing Flux's 30p export peak only cost around 10% of the projected
    year-one return. Under Agile the battery's job flips from <em>exporting at a peak rate</em> to
    <em>not importing at a peak rate</em>, and dodging expensive evening import is worth roughly as
    much as the old peak export was. Predbat reorganised the whole strategy on its own the day we
    swapped the rate sensors over. The only real loss is on true surplus — battery full, house
    satisfied — which now earns 12p instead of 30p.
  </p>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>What we run now</h2></div>
  <table class="spec">
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Import</td><td>Octopus Agile. Half-hourly dynamic pricing, published around 4pm for the next day, capped at £1/kWh. Cheap slots sit in the low teens; evening peaks run from the mid-20s in summer to the high 30s in winter.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Export</td><td>Outgoing Octopus, flat 12p/kWh all day, every day. No export peaks to chase. Boring on purpose, because it's a guaranteed floor.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>VPP</td><td>Axle, events-only, £1/kWh with a £10/month minimum. Settles through the smart meter, so it pays out even without export certification.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="note">
    <p><strong>Export isn't paid yet.</strong> We can't earn anything for export until the final
    installation certification goes in, which can take a month or two from install day. The
    inverter still exports the surplus once the battery's full — we just hand it to the grid for
    free, and the <a href="/stats/">stats</a> honestly report £0 export revenue. On a sunny June
    day that's £2 or more sailing out of the meter for nothing, which is a decent reason to chase
    the paperwork.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note quiet">
    <p><strong>A possible upgrade later.</strong> Agile Outgoing does half-hourly export pricing,
    uncapped, with no exit fees. We modelled a full year of real rates against our own generation:
    it nets about £449/yr versus £466 on flat 12p — effectively a wash, because only the
    16:00–19:00 window beats 12p while this high-generation, low-consumption house floods the grid
    at midday when Agile export pays 5–7p. Flat 12p stays. That comparison is exactly the kind of
    question this site's dataset exists to answer.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>Why Octopus at all?</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    Higher flat export rates do exist elsewhere (E.ON around 16.5p, British Gas around 15p), but
    they all want you importing with the same supplier on a flat or simple time-of-use tariff. For
    a Predbat home the cheap-import arbitrage on Agile is the bigger prize anyway. Octopus also has
    the best API in the business, and the <a href="/stack/">whole automation stack</a> hangs off it.
  </p>

  <style>
    .price-curve {
      display: flex;
      align-items: flex-end;
      gap: 2px;
      height: 190px;
      margin-top: 0.5rem;
    }
    .price-curve .bar { flex: 1; border-radius: 2px 2px 0 0; min-height: 3px; }
    .price-axis {
      display: flex;
      justify-content: space-between;
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.7rem;
      color: var(--ink-400);
      margin-top: 0.4rem;
    }
  </style>
</Base>
